+<section><div class="wrap feature-blocks"><article class="feature-block"><h2>Each option in one paragraph</h2><h3 id="sheetspeed">1. SheetSpeed</h3><p><strong>Best for:</strong> Analysts and modelers who want free formula tracing plus Excel-style shortcuts in Google Sheets and Slides.</p><p>We make SheetSpeed. Trace precedents (Ctrl+Shift+[) is free; trace dependents, goal seek (Alt A W G), what-if tables (Alt A W T) and auto-color are Pro. No ads or tracking inside the extension. <a href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/ljlmgielacploapelnjhodicgbjipcaa" rel="noopener">Chrome Web Store page</a>.</p><h3 id="sheetwhiz">2. SheetWhiz</h3><p><strong>Best for:</strong> Finance teams that want the most-installed option and a long track record (since 2023).</p><p>Pro adds trace precedents/dependents, goal seek, what-if/data sensitivities, paste special, auto-color and format/font/fill cycles. <a href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/bfncmfmdadhimlnpeecppaimgmeeppan" rel="noopener">Chrome Web Store page</a>.</p><h3 id="shortiecuts">3. ShortieCuts</h3><p><strong>Best for:</strong> People who want the widest Office Alt-key coverage with an on-screen Key Tips overlay, including Word shortcuts in Google Docs.</p><p>Also has Accumulate, chart format presets, F4 repeat, Ctrl+9/0 hide. Its privacy page says it logs shortcut names with Google Analytics. <a href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/jbhdbmffapbbghcejohkekfecihgehlc" rel="noopener">Chrome Web Store page</a>.</p><h3 id="spex">4. Spex</h3><p><strong>Best for:</strong> People who want visual on-screen KeyTips and can choose between Excel-style and Sheets-style workflows.</p><p>Young, actively updated project; Quick Access and dark mode. <a href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/gahnolkboklnjhnaahjdkingoejbepcc" rel="noopener">Chrome Web Store page</a>.</p><h3 id="xlkeys">5. XLKeys</h3><p><strong>Best for:</strong> Currently unavailable (checked Oct 18, 2026).</p><p>Currently unavailable (checked Oct 18, 2026). On that date xlkeyz.com returned a 503 deployment pause and its Chrome Web Store listing said not available. We are not saying it shut down. <a href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/jpdneiofokgelbncbjcknpclmgpekllc" rel="noopener">Chrome Web Store page</a>.</p><h3 id="google-sheets-built-in-toggle">6. Google Sheets built-in toggle</h3><p><strong>Best for:</strong> Anyone who only needs common Ctrl shortcuts and can&#x27;t install extensions.</p><p>Covers many Ctrl shortcuts, but no Alt ribbon sequences (Alt H O I, Alt E S V) and no formula tracing. <a href="https://support.google.com/docs/answer/181110" rel="noopener">Google help page</a>.</p></article></div></section>
 <section><div class="wrap feature-blocks"><article class="feature-block"><h2>How to choose</h2><ul><li><strong>Only need common Ctrl shortcuts, or can't install extensions:</strong> turn on Google's compatible spreadsheet shortcuts first. <a href="/blog/how-to-enable-excel-shortcuts-in-google-sheets/">How to enable it</a>.</li><li><strong>Financial modeling on a budget (tracing, goal seek, what-if):</strong> SheetSpeed (tracing is free) or SheetWhiz Pro.</li><li><strong>The most-installed option:</strong> SheetWhiz.</li><li><strong>Widest Alt-key coverage with an on-screen overlay, plus Docs:</strong> ShortieCuts.</li><li><strong>Visual KeyTips in Sheets only:</strong> Spex.</li><li><strong>Work laptop:</strong> ask IT to allowlist the extension by its Chrome Web Store id (SheetSpeed: ljlmgielacploapelnjhodicgbjipcaa) and check each product's permissions before installing.</li></ul><p>Head-to-head pages: <a href="/blog/sheetwhiz-alternative/">SheetSpeed vs SheetWhiz</a> · <a href="/blog/sheetspeed-vs-shortiecuts/">SheetSpeed vs ShortieCuts</a> · <a href="/blog/sheetspeed-vs-spex/">SheetSpeed vs Spex</a> · <a href="/blog/sheetspeed-vs-xlkeys/">SheetSpeed vs XLKeys</a>. Reference: <a href="/blog/google-sheets-shortcuts-list/">Google Sheets shortcuts list (Windows, Mac, Excel equivalents)</a> · <a href="/blog/excel-shortcuts-in-google-sheets-guide/">complete guide to Excel shortcuts in Google Sheets</a>.</p><p><a class="nav-cta" href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/ljlmgielacploapelnjhodicgbjipcaa" rel="noopener" data-store-cta>Add SheetSpeed to Chrome (free)</a></p></article></div></section>
 <section><div class="wrap feature-blocks"><article class="feature-block"><h2>How we checked</h2><p>On October 18, 2026 we opened each product's Chrome Web Store page and its own website and pricing page, and recorded the user count, rating, version, update date, prices and advertised features. We didn't test competitors' paid features. Store numbers change daily, so treat them as a snapshot. If something here is wrong, <a href="https://github.com/jarvis-pd21/sheetspeed-website/issues" rel="noopener">open an issue</a> and we'll fix it.</p></article></div></section>
-<section><div class="wrap feature-blocks"><article class="feature-block"><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>What add-in gives me Excel shortcuts in Google Sheets?</h3><p>The main options are Chrome extensions: SheetSpeed (free Core), SheetWhiz (free plan, Pro $9.99/mo), ShortieCuts (14-day trial, then $7.99/mo), Spex and XLKeys. Google Sheets also has a free built-in toggle: Help → Keyboard shortcuts → Enable compatible spreadsheet shortcuts. Strictly, these are Chrome extensions rather than Google Workspace add-ons, because only an extension can capture Alt key sequences.</p><h3>Which Excel shortcut extension for Google Sheets is free?</h3><p>Google&#x27;s compatible shortcuts toggle is free. SheetSpeed Core is free forever and includes formula tracing (trace precedents). SheetWhiz has a free plan for formatting shortcuts. ShortieCuts is a 14-day free trial.</p><h3>Which one has the most users?</h3><p>SheetWhiz, with 10,000 users and a 4.6-star rating on the Chrome Web Store on Oct 18, 2026. ShortieCuts shows 1,000 users. SheetSpeed and Spex are newer listings.</p><h3>Which extension supports Google Docs and Slides too?</h3><p>ShortieCuts covers Sheets, Slides and Docs. SheetSpeed and SheetWhiz cover Sheets and Slides. Spex covers Sheets only.</p><h3>Do Excel Alt shortcuts like Alt E S V work in Google Sheets?</h3><p>Not natively. Google&#x27;s toggle adds many Ctrl shortcuts but no Alt ribbon sequences. Extensions such as SheetSpeed, SheetWhiz, ShortieCuts and Spex add them. On a Mac, they use Option in place of Alt.</p><h3>Can I trace precedents in Google Sheets?</h3><p>Not natively. SheetSpeed traces precedents for free (Ctrl+Shift+[), and SheetWhiz and ShortieCuts include tracing in their paid plans.</p><h3>Is SheetSpeed the same as sheetspeed.com?</h3><p>No. SheetSpeed, the Excel-shortcuts Chrome extension, lives at sheetspeed.agi-jarvis.com and on the Chrome Web Store (id ljlmgielacploapelnjhodicgbjipcaa). sheetspeed.com is unrelated.</p></article></div></section>
