-<h2>What SheetSpeed does, specifically</h2><p>SheetSpeed is a Chrome extension for Google Sheets (and Google Slides). Install it from the <a href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/ljlmgielacploapelnjhodicgbjipcaa" rel="noopener">Chrome Web Store</a>, open a spreadsheet, and Excel keys start working.</p><p><strong>Excel Alt sequences, tapped like Excel.</strong> Press and release Alt, then type the letters:</p><ul><li><strong>Alt, E, S, V</strong>: paste values. <strong>Alt, E, S, F</strong>: paste formulas. <strong>Alt, E, S, T</strong>: paste formats.</li><li><strong>Alt, H, B</strong> plus a letter: borders.</li><li><strong>Alt, W, F, F</strong>: freeze panes above and left of the selection (Pro).</li></ul><p><strong>Ctrl chords you already know:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Ctrl+Shift+=</strong> inserts rows, <strong>Ctrl+Shift+-</strong> deletes the selected rows.</li><li><strong>Ctrl+Shift+L</strong> toggles a filter.</li><li><strong>Ctrl+Shift+Space</strong> opens a command palette, so you can search any SheetSpeed command by name.</li></ul><p><strong>Trace precedents, free.</strong> Put your cursor on a formula and press <strong>Ctrl+Shift+[</strong>. A panel lists every cell the formula references. Use ↑ and ↓ to move through them, Enter to drill into a nested formula, and F5 to return to where you started. This is the single feature most Excel modelers miss in Sheets, and it's in the free Core.</p><p><strong>Slides, too.</strong> PowerPoint-style shortcuts for ordering, aligning and grouping objects in Google Slides.</p><p><strong>On a Mac</strong>, use Option where Excel uses Alt and ⌘ where it uses Ctrl. Every binding can be changed in SheetSpeed Settings, so if a shortcut clashes with something else in your setup, remap it.</p><p><strong>Privacy.</strong> Core uses the <code>storage</code> and <code>activeTab</code> permissions only. It doesn't ask for Google API (OAuth) access to your Drive or sheets, and it has no analytics package. The privacy policy is <a href="/privacy/">here</a>.</p><p><strong>Pro.</strong> Pro will add trace dependents (Ctrl+Shift+], a bounded scan for every formula that uses a cell), goal seek (Alt, A, W, G), one- or two-variable what-if tables (Alt, A, W, T), freeze panes, auto-color, export as image, font/fill color cycles, and Paste Exact (Ctrl+Alt+Shift+C / V, so references don't shift). Pro tools are free during early access; prices ($7.99/mo, $69/yr, $149 lifetime) start only when billing opens, with notice first. Pro unlocks free in v0.4.0, the next store update. Details are on the <a href="/pricing.html">pricing page</a>.</p><p>Full list of keys: <a href="/blog/excel-shortcuts-list/">SheetSpeed shortcut list</a>.</p>
